# Test: SIGTERM tells the peer WHY before the socket goes
#
# RFC requirement: RFC4271-8.2.2-18 positive -- ManualStop (Event 2) sends the
# NOTIFICATION message with a Cease before the TCP connection is dropped. The
# whole-daemon proof: a real SIGTERM to a real ze holding a real Established
# session, asserted as the bytes the peer reads off the wire, not as a log line.
#
# WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE FILE FROM signal-stop.ci, AND WHAT TO DO ABOUT IT.
# The assertion belongs in signal-stop.ci, which already drives the same signal
# on the same session and asserts only the route, the EOR and a clean exit. It
# could not be put there: adding the RFC tag to that file made it an RFC-tagged
# test, after which .claude/hooks/pretool-writeedit.py refuses every
# behaviour-bearing edit to it without a `rfc-test-change-approved:` marker that
# only the user may write, and the tag and the assertion cannot go in one edit
# because the hook reads the file from disk. Once the user approves, fold the
# expect line below into signal-stop.ci and delete this file
# (plan/spec-fixit-bgp-shutdown-cease-notification.md).
#
# WHAT THIS FILE GUARDS, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT.
# It guards the FEATURE: before 2026-08-10 the daemon sent nothing at all on
# shutdown. Session.Close built exactly this message and had no reachable
# caller, because Peer.cleanup's `if p.session != nil` guard ran after runOnce's
# defer had nil'd p.session, and the socket was already closed by then anyway.
# Delete the send from Reactor.Stop and this file goes red on a bare FIN, with
# the peer holding 2 messages and timing out on the third.
#
# It does NOT guard the ORDERING, and an earlier note here claimed it did. Move
# notifyPeersShutdown after cancel() in Reactor.Stop and this file still passes,
# measured 20/20 on 2026-08-10: the octets are already in the kernel send buffer
# when the cancel goroutine closes the socket, and closeConn sends FIN rather
# than RST, so the peer reads them anyway. The ordering guard is
# TestReactorStopNotifiesWhileItsContextIsStillLive
# (internal/component/bgp/reactor/shutdown_notify_test.go), which observes the
# reactor context from inside the send and is red 50/50 on that revert.

# ze-peer expectations
stdin=peer:terminator=EOF_PEER
option=tcp_connections:value=1

# Connection 1: Static route (192.168.1.0/24) + EOR
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00300200000015400101004002004003040A0000014005040000006418C0A801
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00170200000000

# After initial messages: send SIGTERM to trigger graceful shutdown
action=sigterm:conn=1:seq=2

# The shutdown NOTIFICATION: Cease (code 6) / Administrative Shutdown (subcode
# 2, RFC 4486), carrying the RFC 8203 shutdown communication -- one length octet
# (0x17) and the 23-character default text "Administrative Shutdown" that
# Session.Teardown substitutes for an empty operator message.
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=3:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF002D0306021741646D696E6973747261746976652053687574646F776E
EOF_PEER

# ze bgp configuration (tmpfs required so daemon.pid is written to tmpfs dir)
tmpfs=ze-bgp.conf:terminator=EOF_CONF
bgp {
	peer peer1 {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 1
				remote 1
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}


		update {
			attribute {
				origin igp
				local-preference 100
				next-hop 10.0.0.1
			}
			nlri {
				ipv4/unicast add 192.168.1.0/24
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

# Process orchestration
cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze-peer --port $PORT:stdin=peer
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze start ze-bgp.conf:timeout=20s
